# The forge set — every repository the loop watches.
#
# One `owner/name` per line; `#` starts a comment; blank lines ignored. Read by
# `bin/forge-watch tick --all` and `bin/forge-watch status --all`, which HARD-FAIL if this file is
# missing, empty, or malformed rather than falling back to one repository.
#
# Why a file rather than a default: "watch both repos" was the instruction for a day, it went stale
# the moment a third mattered, and the failure mode of a stale watch set is the one this project keeps
# paying for — a watch reporting both queues clear while covering one of them (quince-devlog#3). The
# implementer side does not need this file: it watches the PRs it opened, so its set is
# self-describing. The architect reviews other people's work, so authorship cannot derive its set, and
# a reviewer's watch set is exactly the thing that must not be a habit.
#
# Adding a repo is a PR against this file, which is the point: the set becomes reviewable, and a
# session that would otherwise have quietly watched less has to be told.
novkostya/quince
novkostya/quince-devlog
